      <Comment>A sulfenic acid is an organosulfur compound and oxoacid with the general formula RSOH. In contrast to |FRAME: Alkyl-Sulfinates sulfinic| and |FRAME: SULFONATES sulfonic| acids, simple sulfenic acids, such as |FRAME: CPD-17426|, are highly reactive and cannot be isolated in solution. Sulfenic acids are fromed in plants of the |FRAME: TAX-4678| genus from |FRAME: S-Alkyl-L-Cysteine-S-Oxides &quot;&lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;-alkyl-L-cysteine S-oxide&quot;| by the action of |FRAME: EC-4.4.1.4| (alliinase). They are acted upon quickly by a second enzyme, the lachrymatory-factor synthase, giving a stable &lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;-oxide product. In the absence of lachrymatory-factor synthase they tend to condense with other sulfenic acid molecules quickly to form |FRAME: CPD-17425 thiosulfinates|.</Comment>
